# Embedded C Style (firmware)

MCU firmware is **C today**. Host `apps/console` is C++. A future C++ firmware
migration is intentional but must be an explicit project — see
`firmware-modules-and-robustness.mdc`. Until then, write and extract in C.

- Use fixed-width types (`uint32_t`, `int32_t`, `int8_t`, ...) everywhere — never
  bare `int`/`long` for register values, sample data, or sizes.
- No dynamic allocation (`malloc`/`new`) anywhere in firmware. Use static storage,
  `.bss`, or the `.dma_buffer` section (see `audio-dsp-conventions.mdc`).
- Never call `HAL_Delay()` from interrupt or DMA-callback context — it deadlocks
  when SysTick can't preempt the caller. See `Audio_Bridge_Start()` in
  `audio_bridge.c`, which busy-waits instead specifically because it can run in
  USB IRQ context.
- Comment the **why**, not the **what** — match the existing style (e.g. the
  RS485 bus-turnaround comments in `main.c`).
- Bounds-check all console/register inputs; reply `ok: ...` / `err: ...` —
  never silently clamp a bad value.

## Function size

Soft cap ~40 lines / one screen for cold-path functions (init, console
handlers, drivers). Split a function when it does more than one job. Prefer
table-driven dispatch (`switches[]`, `pwm_outputs[]` in `main.c`) over growing
an if/else chain when adding new cases.

**Exception — command dispatchers:** `Console_Exec()`'s long if/else-if chain
is an intentional single dispatcher, not a violation of the above. Don't
fragment it into many tiny functions; delegate any branch with real logic to
a well-named helper (`Console_PrintSwitches()`, `Pwm_Apply()`,
`DC_PctToTarget()`), keeping the dispatcher itself a flat list of one-liners.

## SOLID, adapted for embedded C

No classes here (yet), but the ideas still apply:

- **Single Responsibility** — one file = one concern (`cs4304.c` = DAC
  register access, `audio_bridge.c` = sample pipeline, `main.c` = console +
  wiring). Don't let a driver file grow console logic or vice versa.
- **Open/Closed** — extend via data tables and new enum cases
  (`Audio_ChannelMode_t`, `switches[]`) rather than editing existing hot-path
  branches.
- **Interface Segregation** — headers expose only what callers need; mirror
  `audio_bridge.h`'s split between "USB stack facing API" and
  "Console / application API".
- **Dependency Inversion** — console/high-level code calls named setters
  (`Audio_SetToneFreq`, `Audio_SetDCLevel`) — never pokes `SPI2->TXDR` or a
  GPIO register directly from `main.c`; that belongs in the driver/bridge
  layer.

Deliberate file extractions and robustness checklist:
`firmware-modules-and-robustness.mdc`.
